<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Go-Daigo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1288">1288</a>–<a href="/years/1339">1339</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Go-Daigo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Go-Daigo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> reigned from 1318 and plotted to overthrow the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>. Exiled to the Oki islands, he escaped, and in 1333 his supporters destroyed the Hōjō regime, opening the Kenmu Restoration of direct imperial rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-takauji">Ashikaga Takauji</a> turned against him in 1336, and he set up the Southern Court at Yoshino.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-go-daigo.jpg" alt="Emperor Go-Daigo" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Monkan-bō Kōshin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Godaigo_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Go-Daigo was born on 26 November 1288, the second son of Emperor Go-Uda, and before his accession was known by his personal name Takaharu. His mother was Fujiwara no Chushi and his older brother was Emperor Go-Nijo. In 1308, at the death of Go-Nijo, Hanazono took the throne and Takaharu was made crown prince under the direction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> shogunate. His ideal was the Engi era (901-923) under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-daigo">Emperor Daigo</a>, a period of direct imperial rule, and he chose his own posthumous name during his lifetime so as to share it with that emperor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Hanazono abdicated on 29 March 1318 and Go-Daigo succeeded. His plans against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> were discovered in 1324, when the Rokuhara Tandai removed his associate Hino Suketomo in the Shochu Incident, and again in the Genko Incident of 1331, when Yoshida Sadafusa betrayed them. He hid the Sacred Treasures in a castle on Kasagiyama and raised an army, but the castle fell the next year. The shogunate enthroned Emperor Kogon and exiled him to Oki Province, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-toba">Emperor Go-Toba</a> had been sent after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokyu-war">Jokyu War</a> of 1221.</p><p>In 1333 Go-Daigo escaped with the help of Nawa Nagatoshi and raised an army at Senjo Mountain. Ashikaga Takauji, sent to destroy it, changed sides and took the Rokuhara Tandai, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nitta-yoshisada">Nitta Yoshisada</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a>. When it fell, the regent Hojo Takatoki and his family killed themselves, ending Hojo power. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, Go-Daigo began the Kenmu Restoration, but hasty reforms, disputes over land rights and rewards and the exclusion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> from the political order caused complaint. In 1335 Takauji, disaffected, was ordered destroyed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kusunoki-masashige">Kusunoki Masashige</a> proposed reconciliation in 1336, but the emperor refused, and Masashige's army was beaten at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-minatogawa">Battle of Minatogawa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Takauji entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, Go-Daigo fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-hiei">Mount Hiei</a> and, seeking reconciliation, sent the imperial regalia to the Ashikaga side. Takauji enthroned Emperor Komyo and began his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> with the Kenmu Law Code. In January 1337 Go-Daigo escaped the capital, the regalia he had handed over being counterfeit, and set up the Southern Court at Yoshino, which began the period of the Northern and Southern Courts. He sent his sons across the country against the Northern Court. He abdicated on 18 September 1339 in favour of Prince Noriyoshi, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-go-murakami">Emperor Go-Murakami</a>, and died the next day. His overthrow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> shogunate was the last time an emperor held real power until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> of 1868.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Go-Daigo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1333 <b>Emperor Go-Daigo</b> called on local vassals to oppose Hōjō rule in the Kenmu Restoration.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ashikaga-shogunate">Ashikaga shogunate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The power of the Hōjō went unchallenged after the Jōkyū War until 1324, when <b>Emperor Go-Daigo</b> plotted to overthrow them; the plot was discovered almost at once.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kamakura-shogunate">Kamakura shogunate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Emperor Go-Daigo</b> plotted against the Hojo regents for years and was exiled to the Oki islands, but he escaped.</blockquote><a href="/state/kenmu-restoration">Kenmu Restoration</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Emperor Go-Daigo</b> broke with Ashikaga Takauji, Takauji placed Komyo, of the rival Jimyoin branch of the imperial house, on the throne in Kyoto in 1336.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-court">Northern Court</a></li><li><blockquote>After his Kenmu Restoration collapsed, <b>Go-Daigo</b> escaped from Kyoto late in 1336 and established his court at Yoshino in the mountains south of Nara, insisting that the regalia he carried made him the only rightful emperor.</blockquote><a href="/state/southern-court">Southern Court</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kyoto, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Yoshino Angū, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.01,135.77&amp;year=1321" aria-label="Kyoto on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7991 897 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Emperor Go-Daigo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8441 1167h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8441 1167h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.01,135.77&amp;year=1321">What was here in 1321? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
